streams, system calls and assembly
==================================

This document covers io/file.cxx, io/syscall_linux.cxx,
io/string.cxx and the two files in assembly/.  Together they are the
layer the rest of the library stands on: everything above them is
portable C++23, everything here knows the operating system or the
architecture.

The sources are C++23, but every function the headers declare is
defined with C linkage, so the archive exports the C interface and
nothing else; the internal helpers live in an anonymous namespace and
are invisible to a linker.


The streams
-----------

A stream is an opaque object of type stdoc_FILE.  Its layout is an
implementation detail, but internally it is:

    int     fd            operating-system file descriptor
    int     error         sticky failure flag
    int     unbuffered    push every write straight to the kernel
    size_t  length        bytes currently staged
    char    buffer[256]   the staging buffer (STDOC_BUFFER_SIZE)

Three streams exist: stdoc_stdout (fd 1), stdoc_stderr (fd 2) and
stdoc_stdin (fd 0).  stdoc_stdout is buffered; stdoc_stderr is
unbuffered so that it is never reordered with respect to stdout;
stdoc_stdin is reserved for a future input release and is not used by
any function declared in "<libstdoc.h>".

The objects are constant pointers: they stay valid for the whole
program lifetime, but a caller may not rebind stdout to another
object.  A stream bound to a descriptor that does not exist cannot be
obtained through the public interface - test/errors.c builds one on
purpose to exercise what happens next.


Staging
-------

The staging buffer lives inside the stream object, so staging output
performs no allocation.  stdoc_stream_write stages bytes and hands
them to the operating system in a single write when any of three
events happens:

  * the buffer is full,
  * a newline is staged (this is what keeps line-oriented output
    ordered without an exit-time hook, which the freestanding startup
    path does not provide),
  * the write is to an unbuffered stream and the caller has finished.

A payload larger than the buffer is handled by repeated flushes rather
than by an oversized stack copy.

stdoc_stream_flush pushes the staged bytes and clears the buffer; it
succeeds when nothing is staged.  stdoc_stream_discard throws the
staged bytes away without writing them, which is what a failed
formatted call does so that half of a conversion never reaches the
output.

Failure is sticky: once stream->error is set, every further write
fails without touching the system-call layer.


The operating-system layer
--------------------------

io/syscall_linux.cxx is the only place where Linux system-call numbers
and the "-errno in the return register" convention appear.  The
numbers are written as plain constants - no "<asm/unistd.h>" or other
kernel header is consulted, so the build has no dependency outside
this repository - and the architecture picks them from the compiler's
own target, so one without an entry is a compilation error rather than
a silent mixture of two targets:

    aarch64    x86_64    call
    57         3         close
    63         0         read
    64         1         write
    94         231       exit_group

The wrappers are stdoc_sys_write, stdoc_sys_read, stdoc_sys_close and
stdoc_sys_exit.  The first three return 0 on success and -1 on
failure, mirroring the convention documented for the public functions.
A raw kernel result is classified by comparing it, as an unsigned
integer, against -4095: the kernel reports a failure as "-errno" with
errno in 1 .. 4095, so every failure lands in the top 4095 values of
the unsigned range, while every count the kernel completed - however
large - stays below it.  Comparing against the magnitude of the error
instead would read a completed write of 4096 bytes or more as a
failure, which is why that boundary has a test of its own; see
docs/testing.

stdoc_sys_write repeats the system call until the kernel has taken
everything it was given.  A blocking write completes the whole transfer
before it returns, but a short count is a normal result for a terminal
whose output has been stopped and interrupted, for a non-blocking
descriptor, and for anything else that runs out of room mid-transfer,
so a single call reported as success would silently drop the rest of
the buffer.  A result of zero for a request that was not zero is a
failure rather than a retry, since repeating it would never make
progress.

stdoc_sys_read and stdoc_sys_close belong to the platform layer of the
input release that stdoc_stdin is waiting for; nothing in the library
calls them yet.  They are kept next to the write wrapper so that the
whole operating-system surface stays in one file, and they share the
same classification, so a read of 4096 bytes or more is a success
there too.

stdoc_sys_exit uses exit_group rather than exit so that every thread
of the process is terminated.  libstdoc creates no threads, but a
program linked against it may.  It never returns.


The system-call trampolines (assembly/syscall_aarch64.S and
assembly/syscall_x86_64.S)
-------------------------------------------------------------------

The Linux system-call ABI is not the ordinary C calling convention,
and it is not the same on the two architectures this library
implements, so each has a trampoline of its own.

On aarch64 the number is carried in x8 rather than x0, the arguments
in x0..x5 and the result comes back in x0.  Arguments are passed in
the same registers the C convention uses, so the trampoline only has
to shift every argument one register to the right, move the number
into place and execute "svc #0":

    stdoc_syscall:  mov x8, x0        /* number       */
                    mov x0, x1        /* arguments    */
                    ...               /* x0 .. x5     */
                    svc #0
                    ret

On x86_64 the number goes in rax, the result comes back in rax, the
fourth argument goes in r10 - the "syscall" instruction destroys rcx,
the register the C convention uses for that argument - and the sixth
argument is passed on the stack.  The trampoline permutes the argument
registers and then executes "syscall":

    stdoc_syscall:  movq %r8, %r10       /* arg4, not rcx */
                    movq %r9, %r8        /* arg5          */
                    movq 8(%rsp), %r9    /* arg6          */
                    movq %rdi, %rax      /* number        */
                    movq %rsi, %rdi      /* arguments     */
                    ...                  /* rsi, rdx      */
                    syscall
                    ret

The raw kernel return value is returned unmodified, so callers
interpret the "-4095 .. -1" range themselves; the kernel reports a
failure that way on both architectures.  Only registers the C calling
convention already lets a function destroy are touched - x0..x18 on
aarch64, and rax, rcx, rdx, rsi, rdi, r8, r9, r10 and r11 on x86_64 -
so no explicit save or restore is needed.

Written in GNU assembler (GAS) syntax, as the project conventions
require.


The entry points (assembly/start_aarch64.S and
assembly/start_x86_64.S)
-------------------------------------------------

The kernel enters _start with the initial stack layout:

    sp / rsp        the top of the initial stack
    [sp] / [rsp]    argc
    sp / rsp + 8    argv[0], ..., argv[argc], NULL
                    envp[0], ..., NULL

start_aarch64.S clears the frame pointer (x29) and the link register
(x30) so that a stack unwinder stops cleanly at the bottom of the
stack, reads the three values, calls main(argc, argv, envp) and passes
main's return value to stdoc_sys_exit, which becomes the exit status.
start_x86_64.S does the same with the frame pointer (rbp), and aligns
the stack to 16 bytes before the call, as the System V AMD64 ABI
requires.

There is deliberately no more here than the ABI requires: no
constructors, no ".init_array" walk, no thread-local storage and no
atexit handling.  Those facilities are documented as unsupported by
this release, which is also why the formatted functions flush
themselves instead of registering an exit-time flush.


The string helpers (io/string.cxx)
----------------------------------

stdoc_strlen, stdoc_memcpy and stdoc_memset live here because the
library is linked with -nostdlib: it may not call those names from a
host C library, since they would either be missing at link time or
silently resolve to a different implementation than the one under
test.  They are prefixed so they never collide when libstdoc is linked
into a hosted program, and none of them is part of the public API.

The heap allocator in mem/heap.cxx uses stdoc_memcpy to move block
headers in and out of the arena; see docs/heap for why.
